※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⍴` is APL's rho and does APL's two jobs.  Written before one thing it
※ answers that thing's shape; written between two it makes something of the
※ shape on its left out of the values on its right.
※
※ What it answers is what it takes, which is not a coincidence: a number for
※ one dimension and a tuple of numbers for more, so `(⍴a) ⍴ b` is well formed
※ for any array `a`.  A tuple of one is not a thing this language has, which is
※ why one dimension answers the number itself.
※
※ The values fill the new object in the order its elements lie in -- the last
※ dimension moving fastest, which is how they are laid out -- and begin again
※ where they run out.  One value goes everywhere.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let v: u8⟦4⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8, 4u8⟧ in ⎕static
    ※ one value everywhere
    let filled: u8⟦3⟧ = 3 ⍴ 7u8
    ※ fewer values than the shape holds, so they go round again
    let cycled: u8⟦6⟧ = 6 ⍴ ⟦0u8, 1u8⟧
    ※ exactly as many
    let same: u8⟦4⟧ = 4 ⍴ v
    ※ a table, filled in the order its elements lie in
    let m: u8⟦2,3⟧ = 〈2, 3〉 ⍴ v
    ※ a cube, and one value in all of it
    let cube: u16⟦2,2,2⟧ = 〈2, 2, 2〉 ⍴ 9u16
    ※ the shape of one, which is what the left side takes
    let one: u64 = ⍴v
    let two: 〈u64, u64〉 = ⍴m
    let three: 〈u64, u64, u64〉 = ⍴cube
    ※ and what it answers handed straight back to it
    let again: u8⟦2,3⟧ = (⍴m) ⍴ v
    let joined: u8⟦6⟧ = 2 ⍴ 3u8 + 4u8 ⧺ v
    if filled⟦0⟧ ≠ 7u8 ∨ filled⟦2⟧ ≠ 7u8:
        1u6
    elif cycled⟦0⟧ ≠ 0u8 ∨ cycled⟦1⟧ ≠ 1u8 ∨ cycled⟦4⟧ ≠ 0u8 ∨ cycled⟦5⟧ ≠ 1u8:
        2u6
    elif same⟦0⟧ ≠ 1u8 ∨ same⟦3⟧ ≠ 4u8:
        3u6
    ※ the innermost dimension moves quickest, so the second row begins at 4
    elif m⟦0,0⟧ ≠ 1u8 ∨ m⟦0,2⟧ ≠ 3u8 ∨ m⟦1,0⟧ ≠ 4u8 ∨ m⟦1,2⟧ ≠ 2u8:
        4u6
    elif cube⟦0,0,0⟧ ≠ 9u16 ∨ cube⟦1,1,1⟧ ≠ 9u16:
        5u6
    elif one ≠ 4u64:
        6u6
    elif two⟦0⟧ ≠ 2u64 ∨ two⟦1⟧ ≠ 3u64:
        7u6
    elif three⟦0⟧ ≠ 2u64 ∨ three⟦2⟧ ≠ 2u64:
        8u6
    elif again⟦1,2⟧ ≠ 2u8:
        9u6
    ※ it binds tighter than joining and looser than the arithmetic, so this
    ※ is `(2 ⍴ (3u8 + 4u8)) ⧺ v` and not either of the other readings
    elif joined⟦0⟧ ≠ 7u8 ∨ joined⟦1⟧ ≠ 7u8 ∨ joined⟦2⟧ ≠ 1u8 ∨ joined⟦5⟧ ≠ 4u8:
        10u6
    else:
        0u6
